    "This opens in 605 BC, the first of three Babylonian deportations of Judah (605, 597, 586 BC) — Nebuchadnezzar took the brightest young nobles specifically to train them for imperial service, a real, documented Babylonian policy for managing conquered elites. Worth being honest about the human cost underneath the theology: these were most likely teenagers, taken by force from their families and their country, given new names and a new language and a new religious system imposed on them — not adults who volunteered for this. The faithfulness in this chapter is happening under real trauma and real displacement, not from a position of comfort or choice.",
