    "scene": "Picture Nebuchadnezzar waking in the dark, heart pounding, the dream already dissolving even as he reaches for it. He's the most powerful man alive — armies at his word, nations paying him tribute — and none of it means anything against this one thing he cannot control: he cannot remember what terrified him.\n\nHe calls in every wise man in the empire before dawn. And then, in a fit of suspicion nobody sees coming, he raises the stakes past anything reasonable: not only interpret the dream, but tell him what he dreamed first, with no clues given. Fail, and be torn apart. The wise men's own reply is almost pitiable — no one alive can do this, they say, only the gods, and the gods don't live among men.\n\nWord reaches Daniel that the execution squad is already moving through the city, coming for every wise man in Babylon, himself included, over a dream he was never even asked about. He doesn't panic and he doesn't run. He asks for time, and then he does something almost strange for a man facing death by morning: he goes and finds his three friends, and together they pray.",
