    "3:1–7 — A gold image ninety feet tall by nine feet wide, set up on the plain of Dura, and an empire-wide crowd of officials summoned for the dedication. This is liturgy as statecraft: an entire six-instrument orchestra cued to one moment, and when the music plays, every body in the plain drops to the ground at once. The point of an imperial cult was never really theology — it was compliance you could see from a distance.",
    "3:8–12 — The accusation against the three is both political and religious in the same breath: 'these men... have not regarded thee, O king... they serve not thy gods, nor worship the golden image' (3:12). Faithfulness here looks like unemployment and a furnace, not a private disagreement kept quiet.",
    "3:13–18 — Given one more chance, their answer (3:17–18) is the spine of the whole chapter: 'our God whom we serve is able to deliver us... and he will deliver us out of thine hand, O king. But if not, be it known unto thee, O king, that we will not serve thy gods.' Read it slowly — this is not a bargain with God for rescue. It's a refusal to bow either way, rescue or no rescue. Compare Job 13:15 ('though he slay me, yet will I trust in him') and Habakkuk 3:17–18, the same shape of faith stated in advance of the outcome, not after it.",
    "3:19–23 — The furnace is heated seven times hotter, the men are bound in their own clothes and thrown in, and the soldiers who carried them close enough to the opening are killed instantly by the heat (3:22). The text will not let you make the fire metaphorical or symbolic — real men die from its heat in the very same verses where the three men do not.",
