  "slug": "daniel",
  "chapterLabel": "Chapter 4 — Nebuchadnezzar's Madness",
  "chNum": "4",
  "points": [
    "4:1–3 — The entire chapter is framed as Nebuchadnezzar's own royal proclamation, sent 'unto all people, nations, and languages, that dwell in all the earth' (4:1) — the pagan emperor of the known world personally writing and circulating an empire-wide testimony about the God who humbled him. No other chapter in Daniel lets the king preach the last paragraph in his own voice from the opening verse to the closing one.",
    "4:4–9 — Nebuchadnezzar is 'at rest' and 'flourishing' in his own palace (4:4) when another dream troubles him. His own wise men fail again, exactly as in chapter 2 — until 'Daniel came in before me, whose name was Belteshazzar, according to the name of my god' (4:8). Even the king's own language admits the tension the whole book runs on: Daniel carries a Babylonian name built around a Babylonian god, and serves the true God anyway.",
    "4:10–12 — The dream: a tree of enormous height, visible 'to the end of all the earth,' feeding all flesh and sheltering every beast and bird. Ezekiel had already used this exact image for Assyria's proud king (Ezekiel 31:3–14) — a nation Babylon itself had swallowed. Pride, across Scripture, keeps reaching for the same picture: a tree too big to imagine falling.",
    "4:13–17 — 'A watcher and an holy one' descends from heaven and issues the sentence: cut the tree down, but leave the stump bound in the ground — and then the verse that is really the point of the whole chapter: 'to the intent that the living may know that the most High ruleth in the kingdom of men, and giveth it to whomsoever he will, and setteth up over it the basest of men' (4:17). That is Daniel 2:21 and 4:17 and 5:21 all making the identical claim in three different chapters — worth saying that plainly to your group so the repetition itself registers as the point.",
    "4:18–27 — Daniel — called Belteshazzar here — is 'astonied for one hour' before he can bring himself to answer (4:19), and Daniel's own reluctance is worth sitting with: 'my lord, the dream be to them that hate thee' — he is not eager to watch a king fall, even a pagan one, even after everything this king has done. The interpretation: the tree is Nebuchadnezzar himself, and the sentence is real madness — seven 'times' living like a beast — until he 'know that the most High ruleth.' Daniel's counsel afterward, 'break off thy sins by righteousness... if it may be a lengthening of thy tranquillity' (4:27), leaves the king an actual window to repent before judgment falls; the text seems to assume roughly a year passes before it does.",
    "4:28–33 — Twelve months later, walking on his own palace roof, the king's pride speaks for itself: 'is not this great Babylon, that I have built... by the might of my power, and for the honour of my majesty?' (4:29–30). The judgment falls mid-sentence — 'while the word was in the king's mouth' (4:31) — a detail worth reading aloud exactly as written. He is driven from human society, eats grass like cattle, and his hair and nails grow wild (4:33). Delay, across this whole book, is never the same thing as cancellation.",
    "4:34–37 — Restoration comes the instant he 'lifted up mine eyes unto heaven' (4:34) — reason and kingdom are given back together, in the same motion. His closing words are the moral he writes for himself: 'those that walk in pride he is able to abase' (4:37). Hold this chapter next to Revelation 18's Babylon, which gets no chapter-4 ending — no roof, no seven years, no grass, no restoration, only a fall (Revelation 18:21). Two Babylons, two very different endings, same word."
  ],
