  "chapterLabel": "Chapter 6 — The Lions' Den",
  "chNum": "6",
  "points": [
    "6:1–3 — Daniel is set over the whole kingdom under Darius specifically because of 'an excellent spirit' in him (6:3) — the king's plan is to promote him over the entire realm. Worth noting the continuity across nearly seventy years and at least four kings (Nebuchadnezzar, Belshazzar, Darius, soon Cyrus): Daniel's excellence is never a young man's flash, it's a whole lifetime of the same character.",
    "6:4–9 — The other officials search Daniel's record for any fault or corruption and find nothing (6:4) — so they manufacture a law instead of a crime: no prayer to anyone but the king for thirty days, on pain of the lions' den, sealed as an irrevocable Medo-Persian statute (6:7–9). The trap here is entirely legal, not moral. That distinction matters: the system itself, not a personal grudge, is the weapon.",
    "6:10 — Daniel's response is deliberately unremarkable: he goes home, opens his windows toward Jerusalem exactly as he 'did aforetime,' and prays three times a day, exactly as before. The chapter is not staging a new act of heroism here — it's showing that faithfulness under a death sentence looks identical to faithfulness on any ordinary day. Compare Solomon's dedication prayer, which specifically anticipated exiles praying toward Jerusalem (1 Kings 8:46–49) — Daniel is keeping a practice built centuries earlier for exactly this situation, not inventing a new piety under pressure.",
    "6:11–15 — Darius, once he understands what's happened, is 'sore displeased with himself' and works until sundown trying to find a way to save Daniel (6:14) — but Medo-Persian law, once signed, 'altereth not' (6:8, 15). This is worth naming plainly to your group: real political power can be genuinely trapped inside its own system. Darius is not a villain here; he's a king who signed away his own authority without realizing it.",
    "6:16–18 — The king's own words as Daniel is thrown in are remarkable coming from a pagan ruler: 'thy God whom thou servest continually, he will deliver thee' (6:16). The den is sealed with both the king's signet and his lords' signets (6:17) — nobody, including Darius, can tamper with it alone. Darius spends the night fasting, refusing entertainment, unable to sleep (6:18) — the king suffers something like Daniel's own vigil while Daniel is in the den.",
    "6:19–23 — At dawn, the king runs — not walks — to the den, and cries out 'with a lamentable voice' (6:20) before he even knows the answer. Daniel's own explanation is worth reading exactly as given: 'my God hath sent his angel, and hath shut the lions' mouths... forasmuch as before him innocency was found in me; and also before thee, O king, have I done no hurt' (6:22). Notice what Daniel claims: not cleverness, not luck, but innocence before both God and the king — a clean record in both directions.",
    "6:24 — The accusers, along with their wives and children, are thrown to the same lions and are killed instantly, before even reaching the bottom of the den — a detail meant to underline how total and immediate the lions' hunger was, making Daniel's own survival even more plainly supernatural. The collective family penalty reflects real Ancient Near Eastern legal practice of the era; it isn't a model Scripture is holding up for imitation, it's simply how that world's justice worked.",
    "6:25–28 — Darius's decree afterward — that all his subjects should 'tremble and fear before the God of Daniel... his kingdom that which shall not be destroyed' (6:26) — is one more pagan king independently arriving at Daniel 2:44's own language. Court stories in this book keep ending with foreign rulers preaching the book's own thesis back to the reader. The chapter closes noting Daniel prospered 'in the reign of Darius, and in the reign of Cyrus the Persian' (6:28) — bridging directly into the political world of chapters 9–10."
  ],
