    "9:1–2 — In the first year of Darius the Mede, Daniel is doing something worth naming specifically: reading Scripture. He 'understood by books the number of the years... that he would accomplish seventy years in the desolations of Jerusalem' (9:2), a number Jeremiah had given decades earlier (Jeremiah 25:11–12; 29:10). The entire chapter's prophecy grows directly out of Daniel taking an earlier prophet's plain words seriously and doing the arithmetic himself — real Bible study before it becomes real prayer.",
    "9:3–19 — What follows is almost entirely confession, not petition: 'we have sinned, and have committed iniquity' (9:5), repeated in various forms across seventeen verses. Daniel includes himself in a generation that wasn't even alive for the worst of the covenant unfaithfulness he's confessing (9:5–11) — corporate repentance for sins he personally didn't commit. The prayer's turn, in 9:18–19, is entirely on God's own character, not Israel's merit: 'we do not present our supplications before thee for our righteousnesses, but for thy great mercies... for thine own sake, O my God.'",
    "9:20–23 — Gabriel arrives 'about the time of the evening oblation' (9:21) — a striking detail, since there is no standing temple or functioning sacrifice in Babylon at this moment. The exile has ended the ritual, but Daniel's own internal clock still keeps its time. Gabriel's opening line matters as much as anything that follows: 'at the beginning of thy supplications the commandment came forth... for thou art greatly beloved' (9:23) — the answer was already in motion before Daniel finished praying.",
    "9:24 — 'Seventy weeks are determined upon thy people and upon thy holy city' — the addressee is explicitly Israel and Jerusalem, a detail worth reading aloud, since it's exactly why this verse became the fork the whole chapter turns on. Six purposes are packed into one sentence: finish the transgression, make an end of sins, make reconciliation for iniquity, bring in everlasting righteousness, seal up the vision and prophecy, and anoint the most holy. Ask your group directly which of these six they think are already accomplished and which, if any, are still pending — that question alone previews the whole doctrinal fork.",
    "9:25 — From 'the going forth of the commandment to restore and to build Jerusalem' to 'Messiah the Prince' is measured as seven weeks plus sixty-two weeks — sixty-nine sevens total. Which decree starts this clock (Cyrus's, or one of Artaxerxes' later decrees in Ezra 7 or Nehemiah 2) is itself disputed among careful readers, and the answer changes exactly where the sixty-ninth week lands historically.",
    "9:26 — 'After threescore and two weeks shall Messiah be cut off, but not for himself' — the verb is genuinely violent, describing something done to a messianic figure, and it happens before 'the people of the prince that shall come' destroy 'the city and the sanctuary' — read by most as a clear advance description of Jerusalem's fall, whether at the cross's aftermath in AD 70 (most readers) or as its own separate future event (a minority within the dispensational camp).",
    "9:27 — The last week: 'he shall confirm the covenant with many for one week: and in the midst of the week he shall cause the sacrifice and the oblation to cease, and for the overspreading of abominations he shall make it desolate.' This single verse generates more competing charts than any other sentence in Daniel. Read it slowly to your group, name the views below, and resist the urge to finish the sentence for them — the identity of 'he' (Messiah, from verse 26? a future ruler, distinct from Messiah?) is precisely what the four readings below disagree about."
