  "doctrinal": "This is the hottest text in the whole book. You may say plainly that you read a future seventieth week — that's a defensible, careful reading. Then put the other three readings on the table with their own verses, so the group hears that devout, careful people land in different places from the very same grammar. Six purposes are named in 9:24; you don't have to resolve all six tonight to still teach this chapter honestly.",
  "story": {
    "scene": "Picture Daniel, alone with a scroll — Jeremiah's actual words, seventy years for the desolation of Jerusalem to end. He's counted the years himself. They're almost up. And instead of simply waiting it out, he does something that costs him something: he puts on sackcloth, sits in ashes, and prays one of the most searing confessions in all of Scripture — not for his own sins particularly, but for his whole people's, generations of them, going back centuries.\n\nMid-prayer, before he's even finished speaking, the angel Gabriel arrives — sent, he says, the moment Daniel began praying, because Daniel is greatly beloved. What follows is one of the most numerically dense, fiercely argued prophecies in the whole Bible: seventy weeks, decreed, counted out to the exact turning point of history.\n\nDaniel asked for an ending. He was given a timeline instead, one that reaches centuries past his own lifetime.",
    "modern": "Picture someone who reads history seriously enough to know exactly how bad things have gotten before, and how long recoveries have taken — not naive optimism, but real, sober awareness of the cost.\n\nInstead of either despairing or waiting passively, they get specific: naming exactly what's wrong, taking real responsibility for their own part in it, not just other people's failures, and asking honestly for things to actually change.\n\nThe answer that comes isn't usually instant relief. It's often a clearer sense of the road still ahead — which, strangely, is its own kind of comfort."
  },
