    "12:1 — 'At that time shall Michael stand up' — continuing directly from chapter 11's final king — and a time of trouble comes 'such as never was since there was a nation.' Deliverance is promised specifically to 'every one that shall be found written in the book' (12:1). Revelation 12 and 20 will not be able to write about the end without reaching back to this exact paragraph — Michael standing to fight, unprecedented tribulation, and a book of names.",
    "12:2 — 'Many of them that sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt' — this is the Old Testament's clearest statement of a resurrection that divides into two outcomes, not one universal fate. Jesus doesn't introduce this idea in John 5:28–29 ('all that are in the graves shall hear his voice... resurrection of life... resurrection of damnation') — he's building directly on this verse, six centuries earlier.",
    "12:3 — 'They that be wise shall shine as the brightness of the firmament; and they that turn many to righteousness as the stars for ever and ever.' Not a proverb about education or cleverness — read in context, this is specifically what remains, visibly, after the trouble of verse 1 has passed.",
    "12:4 — 'Shut up the words, and seal the book, even to the time of the end' — worth setting directly beside Revelation 22:10, where John is told the opposite: 'seal not the sayings of the prophecy of this book: for the time is at hand.' Two nearly opposite commands, centuries apart, are themselves a clue about how each book understood its own moment in the story.",
    "12:5–7 — Two more figures appear, and one asks the question every reader of this book eventually asks out loud: 'how long shall it be to the end of these wonders?' (12:6). The answer comes as a sworn oath — 'by him that liveth for ever' — and gives the exact same fraction as Daniel 7:25: 'a time, times, and an half' (12:7), tied to when 'he shall have accomplished to scatter the power of the holy people.' A limited, bounded war against God's people, not an open-ended one — the same phrase Revelation 12:14 will use for the woman protected in the wilderness.",
    "12:8–10 — Daniel himself admits plainly, 'I heard, but I understood not' (12:8), and asks for the outcome directly: 'O my Lord, what shall be the end of these things?' The answer he actually receives is not a fuller explanation — it's a redirection: 'go thy way, Daniel: for the words are closed up and sealed till the time of the end... none of the wicked shall understand; but the wise shall understand' (12:9–10). Understanding itself, this verse says, is part of what gets refined through the trouble, not handed out in advance to everyone equally.",
    "12:11–12 — Two more numbers, tied to 'the time that the daily sacrifice shall be taken away, and the abomination that maketh desolate set up': 1,290 days, then a blessing pronounced on whoever 'waiteth, and cometh to the thousand three hundred and five and thirty days' (12:11–12). Neither number equals the 1,260 days (three and a half years) implied by 12:7's 'time, times, and an half' — don't force them to match. Put all three numbers on the table honestly and let the extra thirty and forty-five days stay a genuine, unresolved mystery rather than inventing a confident explanation for your group.",
